  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.2. Increased institutional capacity of workers&#x2019; organizations</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Palestinian General Federation for Trade Unions (PGFTU) has for the first time formulated and adopted by end of December 2023 a Gender Equality Strategy (2023-2028). The strategy aims at prioritizing key issues, such as improving working conditions, creating new opportunities for women through skills development, and investing in workplace childcare facilities.

PGFTU jointly with representatives of Ministry of Labour, Federation of Palestinian Chambers of Commerce, Industry and Agriculture, Ministry of Women, General Union of Palestinian Women, and Civil Society Coalition have developed a plan of action for establishing a National Committee for Pay Equity with the aim of promoting gender equality and ensuring equal pay for equal work.

PGFTU through the ILO technical and financial support  conducted field visits in various governorates to work sites in the private education, agro-food industry, administrative works, health, construction, and tourism sectors, through which the federation reached more than 1000 workers mainly women workers. As a result, around new 500 workers were organized mainly women, and offered capacity building on labour rights stipulated in the Palestinian labour law, trade union principles and roles, and International Labour Standards (ILS) mainly C 190, C 98, C87, C 100 , C156, C 183, and C111. Moreover, PGFTU supported to establish new 35 worker&#x2019;s committees in several worksites, a national negotiation team comprise of 24 members, half of which are women assigned to support negotiation and collective bargaining at national and enterprises levels, and launching a new electronic membership and archiving systems to simplify affiliation and keep track of new members. The ILO has provided support to the Palestinian General Federation of Trade Unions (PGFTU) as a key partner in the Palestine. The ILO provided training to equip the unions with knowledge and skills essential for implementing the gender equality strategy effectively. 

Moreover, the ILO&apos;s international expertise has been instrumental in reviewing and refining the strategy, ensuring it aligns with global gender equality conventions. This support has strengthened the PGFTU&apos;s capacity and awareness, enhancing the workers&apos; understanding of trade union work and organizations through targeted awareness-raising activities. 

Additionally, the ILO provided technical assistance, helping improve the PGFTU&apos;s structures, role, and functions to adhere to international trade union principles, fostering a more effective and inclusive trade union movement in the region.
The ILO&apos;s technical support has been vital in establishing and bolstering the Pay Equity committee. Drawing on its vast experience in pay equity worldwide, the ILO transferred best practices and technical expertise to raise awareness among social partners on the importance of pay equity. 

Through capacity-building training, the ILO has strengthened the PGFTU&apos;s collective bargaining capabilities and social dialogue skills at the enterprise level. Workers have been equipped with the knowledge and tools necessary for effective negotiations, fostering constructive dialogue between employers and employees. This capacity building has contributed to promoting fair labour practices and improving working conditions.</narrative>
   </description>
